  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of Homeland Security has obligated $58.7B in contract dollars in FY2026 to date. Obligations are up 107.2% year over year on 22,816 awards, with an average award size of $2.6M. The FY series shows growth from $19.7B in FY21 to $28.3B in FY25, with the current fiscal-year-to-date total at $58.7B. Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the largest NAICS category at $28.5B, followed by Ship Building and Repairing at $7.7B and Security Guards and Patrol Services at $3.5B. The top vendors show heavy concentration in construction and shipbuilding, led by FISHER SAND & GRAVEL CO with $13.4B and BARNARD CONSTRUCTION COMPANY, INCORPORATED with $4.5B. All figures are contract obligations and current-year totals are partial (fiscal year to date). This snapshot highlights where DHS is directing its contracting dollars, how many awards the agency is managing, and which firms and industries account for the largest shares of spending in FY2026 to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How much has the Department of Homeland Security obligated so far in FY2026?",
        "body": "The Department of Homeland Security has obligated $58.7B in FY2026 to date. Obligations are up 107.2% year over year on 22,816 awards, with an average award size of $2.6M. The FY trend shows a rise from $19.7B in FY21 to $28.3B in FY25, then to $58.7B in FY26. Current-year totals are partial and reported fiscal year to date."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors receive the largest DHS contract dollars?",
        "body": "Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is the largest industry at $28.5B, followed by Ship Building and Repairing at $7.7B and Security Guards and Patrol Services at $3.5B. FISHER SAND & GRAVEL CO is the top vendor with $13.4B, followed by BARNARD CONSTRUCTION COMPANY, INCORPORATED at $4.5B and DAVIE DEFENSE INC. at $3.5B. The top ten vendors include several construction and shipbuilding firms that together account for a large share of FY2026 to date obligations."
      }
    ]
